    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.ebed818d8db4a8f0149f",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "For example, by aggregating the data in a month, you can determine that August 2017 was, on average, warmer than the year before. Instead, to see which month had the highest temperature, you'd compare the maximum temperature for each month.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Introduction to time series",
        "Aggregating time series"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/fundamentals/timeseries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "aaa0df85d3c9a6ee56133f232af61ea75f549ce0",
        "sha256": "91cb08c8677efd98f7a5d0d1853fc98b712f1ea37e4e5dec5fb499108e697400",
        "lineStart": 65,
        "lineEnd": 65,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/fundamentals/timeseries/index.md#L65-L65"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.558c6d1d0159cd4d489f",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "How you choose to aggregate your time series data is an important decision and depends on the story you want to tell with your data. It's common to use different aggregations to visualize the same time series data in different ways.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Introduction to time series",
        "Aggregating time series"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/fundamentals/timeseries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "aaa0df85d3c9a6ee56133f232af61ea75f549ce0",
        "sha256": "91cb08c8677efd98f7a5d0d1853fc98b712f1ea37e4e5dec5fb499108e697400",
        "lineStart": 67,
        "lineEnd": 67,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/fundamentals/timeseries/index.md#L67-L67"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.09a1b823cf6587f476b2",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "In the IT industry, time series data is often collected to monitor things like infrastructure, hardware, or application events. Machine-generated time series data is typically collected with short intervals, which allows you to react to any unexpected changes, moments after they occur. As a consequence, data accumulates at a rapid pace, making it vital to have a way to store and query data efficiently. As a result, databases optimized for time series data have seen a rise in popularity in recent years.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Introduction to time series",
        "Time series and monitoring"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/fundamentals/timeseries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "aaa0df85d3c9a6ee56133f232af61ea75f549ce0",
        "sha256": "91cb08c8677efd98f7a5d0d1853fc98b712f1ea37e4e5dec5fb499108e697400",
        "lineStart": 71,
        "lineEnd": 71,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/fundamentals/timeseries/index.md#L71-L71"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.ce643ea935d288fa875f",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "A time series database (TSDB) is a database explicitly designed for time series data. While it's possible to use any regular database to store measurements, a TSDB comes with some useful optimizations.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Introduction to time series",
        "Time series and monitoring",
        "Time series databases"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/fundamentals/timeseries/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "aaa0df85d3c9a6ee56133f232af61ea75f549ce0",
        "sha256": "91cb08c8677efd98f7a5d0d1853fc98b712f1ea37e4e5dec5fb499108e697400",
        "lineStart": 75,
        "lineEnd": 75,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/fundamentals/timeseries/index.md#L75-L75"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
